First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x_0&=\sqrt{\left(1\right) + 1} \\ &=\sqrt{2} \\ y_0&=- \left(1\right)^{3} - 3 \\ &=-4
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x'(t)&=\frac{1}{2 \sqrt{t + 1}} \\ y'(t)&=- 3 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=\frac{y\prime(1)}{x\prime(1)}&=\frac{-3}{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}}=- 6 \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form with $(\sqrt{2},-4)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y+4&=- 6 \sqrt{2}\left(x-\sqrt{2}\right) \\ y&=- 6 \sqrt{2} x + 12+\left(-4\right) \\ y&=- 6 \sqrt{2} x + 8
\end{aligned}
$$

First evaluate the coordinates at $t=-1$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x_0&=2 \left(-1\right)^{5} - 3 \left(-1\right)^{3} \\ &=1 \\ y_0&=- 2 \left(-1\right)^{2} \\ &=-2
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x'(t)&=10 t^{4} - 9 t^{2} \\ y'(t)&=- 4 t
\end{aligned}
$$

The tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=\frac{y\prime(-1)}{x\prime(-1)}&=\frac{4}{1}=4
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form with $(1,-2)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y+2&=4\left(x-1\right) \\ y&=4 x - 4+\left(-2\right) \\ y&=4 x - 6
\end{aligned}
$$

First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x_0&=2 \left(1\right)^{5} - \left(1\right)^{3} \\ &=1 \\ y_0&=- \left(1\right)^{2} \\ &=-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x'(t)&=10 t^{4} - 3 t^{2} \\ y'(t)&=- 2 t
\end{aligned}
$$

The tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=\frac{y\prime(1)}{x\prime(1)}&=\frac{-2}{7}=- \frac{2}{7}
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form with $(1,-1)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y+1&=- \frac{2}{7}\left(x-1\right) \\ y&=- \frac{2 x}{7} + \frac{2}{7}+\left(-1\right) \\ y&=- \frac{2 x}{7} - \frac{5}{7}
\end{aligned}
$$

First evaluate the coordinates at $t=-1$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x_0&=- 2 \left(-1\right)^{2} + 2 \left(-1\right) \\ &=-4 \\ y_0&=- \left(-1\right)^{5} \\ &=1
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x'(t)&=- 4 t + 2 \\ y'(t)&=- 5 t^{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

The tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=\frac{y\prime(-1)}{x\prime(-1)}&=\frac{-5}{6}=- \frac{5}{6}
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form with $(-4,1)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-1&=- \frac{5}{6}\left(x+4\right) \\ y&=- \frac{5 x}{6} - \frac{10}{3}+\left(1\right) \\ y&=- \frac{5 x}{6} - \frac{7}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x_0&=\sqrt{\left(1\right) + 3} \\ &=2 \\ y_0&=3 \left(1\right)^{3} + 4 \\ &=7
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x'(t)&=\frac{1}{2 \sqrt{t + 3}} \\ y'(t)&=9 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=\frac{y\prime(1)}{x\prime(1)}&=\frac{9}{\frac{1}{4}}=36
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form with $(2,7)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-7&=36\left(x-2\right) \\ y&=36 x - 72+\left(7\right) \\ y&=36 x - 65
\end{aligned}
$$
